---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Layout Algorithm">
  <p>
    ArrowCrab uses a variant of
    <strong>Sugiyama layered graph drawing</strong> with orthogonal edge
    routing on a unified integer grid. Nodes and edges share the same coordinate
    system, so edges can't cross through nodes.<sup><a href="#fn1">1</a></sup>
  </p>

  <h2>Phase 1: Layer Assignment</h2>
  <p>
    Each node gets a horizontal layer using <strong
      >as-late-as-possible (ALAP)</strong
    > scheduling. The algorithm walks nodes in reverse topological order and
    computes each node's distance to the nearest sink. Nodes go at layer <code
      >maxDepth - distanceToSink</code
    >.
  </p>
  <p>
    ALAP over ASAP because it keeps connected nodes closer together, which
    means fewer long edges that need vertical lane reservations.
  </p>

  <h3>Inputs and Outputs</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr
        ><td>Input</td><td>Node IDs + successor adjacency</td><td
          >The scope's DAG structure</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>Output</td><td><code>layers: number[][]</code></td><td
          >Nodes grouped by layer</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>Output</td><td><code>layerOf: Map</code></td><td
          >Reverse lookup: node &rarr; layer</td
        ></tr
      >
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Phase 2: Ordering Within Layers</h2>
  <p>
    Minimizes edge crossings with a <strong
      >two-pass barycenter heuristic</strong
    >:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Forward pass</strong> (top to bottom): sort each layer by the average
      position of its predecessors in the layer above.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Backward pass</strong> (bottom to top): sort each layer by the average
      position of its successors in the layer below.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    This is a standard heuristic that removes most crossings in one
    forward+backward sweep. A single iteration is enough for the graph sizes
    ArrowCrab handles.
  </p>

  <h2>Phase 3: Routing Area Sizing</h2>
  <p>
    Between every pair of adjacent layers (and above the first / below the
    last), the algorithm reserves a <strong>routing area</strong>, which is a
    horizontal band of grid rows for edge lanes. The height of each routing area
    depends on how many edges need a horizontal turn there:
  </p>
  <pre><code>height = max(MIN_CHANNEL_SIZE, ROUTING_PADDING + edgeCount * LANE_SPACING + ROUTING_PADDING)</code></pre>
  <p>
    This makes sure there are always enough rows for edges to route without
    overlapping, while keeping gaps small where few edges cross.
  </p>

  <h2>Phase 4: Grid Construction and Node Placement</h2>
  <p>
    A sparse grid (<code>LayoutGrid</code>) is constructed with dual storage:
    node occupancy is recorded as axis-aligned rectangles in a <code
      >NodeRect[]</code
    >
    array (O(1) per node regardless of area), while routing lane marks use a
    <code>Map&lt;number, CellOccupant&gt;</code> with
    <code>col * ROW_MULTIPLIER + row</code> keys (where <code
      >ROW_MULTIPLIER</code
    >
    is <code>floor(sqrt(MAX_SAFE_INTEGER))</code>). Layers and routing areas
    interleave:
  </p>
  <pre><code>[routing area 0]      horizontal edge lanes
[layer 0 nodes]       nodes placed left to right
[routing area 1]      horizontal edge lanes
[layer 1 nodes]
...
[routing area N]      final horizontal edge lanes</code></pre>
  <p>
    Within each layer, nodes are placed left to right with horizontal gaps sized
    to accommodate vertical edge lanes between them. All node cells are marked
    as occupied on the grid. Node widths are computed from the maximum of: port
    count, label width, and <code>MIN_NODE_WIDTH</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Phase 5: Edge Routing</h2>
  <p>
    Edges are processed <strong>longest first</strong> (more layers spanned means
    higher priority for lane picks). Each edge becomes an orthogonal polyline:
  </p>

  <h3>Short Edge (Adjacent Layers)</h3>
  <pre><code>source port &rarr; down to horizontal lane &rarr; across to target column &rarr; down to target port</code></pre>
  <p>4 waypoints.</p>

  <h3>Long Edge (2+ Layers)</h3>
  <pre><code>source port &rarr; down to horizontal lane &rarr; across to vertical lane
&rarr; down through intermediate layers &rarr; across to target column &rarr; down to target port</code></pre>
  <p>6 waypoints.</p>

  <h3>Lane Selection</h3>
  <p>
    Horizontal lanes are assigned sequentially with a per-area counter, so each
    edge gets its own row in the routing area.
    <code>findUnusedVerticalLane</code> scans columns outward from the midpoint between
    source and target looking for a free vertical span. Each used cell gets
    marked occupied so later edges skip it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Long edges go first because they need vertical lanes spanning many rows,
    which makes them hardest to place. Giving them first pick leads to cleaner
    layouts.
  </p>

  <h3>Crossing Detection</h3>
  <p>
    After routing, the algorithm finds where edge segments cross. Since all
    edges are orthogonal, crossings only happen between horizontal and vertical
    segments. It collects all H and V segments, sorts verticals by x, then for
    each horizontal segment binary-searches for verticals in its x-range and
    checks y-overlap. This is O((S + K) log S) where S is segments and K is
    crossings, instead of O(E&sup2;) brute-force. The renderer draws a small
    hollow circle at each crossing so you can tell overlapping paths from
    connected ones.
  </p>

  <h2>Phase 6: Pixel Conversion</h2>
  <p>
    Grid-unit coordinates become pixel coordinates by multiplying by <code
      >GRID_UNIT</code
    > (12 px). For nested scopes, absolute pixel offsets from the parent are
    applied during routing, not layout. This keeps each scope's layout
    independent.
  </p>

  <hr />

  <h2>Compound Nodes</h2>
  <p>
    Expanded compound nodes contain child subgraph scopes. Layout goes
    <strong>bottom-up</strong>: deepest scopes first, then parent node sizes
    come from child grid sizes:
  </p>
  <pre><code>width  = max(leafWidth, sum(childWidths) + separators + padding)
height = max(childHeights) + LABEL_BAR_HEIGHT + NODE_INNER_GAP * 2</code></pre>
  <p>
    Collapsed compound nodes shrink to standard leaf node size. Expanded ones
    show the full child layout inside, and the parent scope re-layouts to fit.
  </p>

  <h2>Grid-Aligned Ports</h2>
  <p>
    Ports are placed at <strong>integer grid coordinates</strong>:
  </p>
  <pre><code>port i &rarr; PORT_SPACING * i + PORT_SPACING / 2 = 2i + 1</code></pre>
  <p>
    This gives positions 1, 3, 5, 7, ... relative to the node's left column.
    Since ports are on the same grid as edges, routing lanes connect to ports
    without fractional offsets. Node width is at least
    <code>max(portCountIn, portCountOut) * PORT_SPACING</code> to fit all ports.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Boundary ports</strong> (scope entry/exit points) are different: they use
    centered proportional spacing across the full frame width, since the frame
    can be much wider than the port count needs.
  </p>

  <hr />

  <h2>Key Invariants</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>No edge-node intersection.</strong> Node cells are marked occupied before
      routing. Lane-finding functions only return unoccupied cells.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No edge-edge overlap.</strong> Each lane cell is marked used after assignment.
      Subsequent edges skip occupied cells.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Grid alignment.</strong> All positions and waypoints sit at integer
      grid-unit coordinates.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Routing area sufficiency.</strong> Each routing area has at least as
      many lane rows as edges crossing that gap.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Bottom-up compound sizing.</strong> Child scope grids are computed before
      parent scopes, so parent node dimensions are always correct.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Constants Reference</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Name</th><th>Value</th><th>Unit</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr
        ><td><code>GRID_UNIT</code></td><td>12</td><td>px</td><td
          >Pixels per grid unit</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td><code>NODE_HORIZONTAL_PADDING</code></td><td>2</td><td>GU</td><td
          >Padding on each side of a node</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td><code>ROUTING_PADDING</code></td><td>1</td><td>GU</td><td
          >Padding above/below lane area</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td><code>MIN_CHANNEL_SIZE</code></td><td>3</td><td>GU</td><td
          >Minimum routing area height</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td><code>LANE_SPACING</code></td><td>1</td><td>GU</td><td
          >Distance between lane rows</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td><code>PORT_SPACING</code></td><td>2</td><td>GU</td><td
          >Distance between port centers</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td><code>MIN_NODE_WIDTH</code></td><td>4</td><td>GU</td><td
          >Minimum node width</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td><code>LEAF_NODE_HEIGHT</code></td><td>3</td><td>GU</td><td
          >Height of leaf nodes</td
        ></tr
      >
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <hr />
  <p id="fn1" style="font-size: 0.85em; color: #999;">
    <sup>1</sup> Some refinements were informed by "Method for Orthogonal Edge Routing of Directed Layered
    Graphs with Edge Crossings Reduction" (2021).
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
